Bundled / linked third-party software
================================================================================

This distribution includes the following third-party software:

* PGlite — Apache-2.0 — https://github.com/electric-sql/pglite
  Postgres compiled to WebAssembly. Embedded into the binary.

* @huggingface/transformers — Apache-2.0 — https://github.com/huggingface/transformers.js
  ONNX runtime inference for embedding models. Loaded lazily at first use.

* Hugging Face model: Xenova/multilingual-e5-small — MIT — https://huggingface.co/intfloat/multilingual-e5-small
  Downloaded at first use to ~/.piper/cache/models/ if WASM embedding mode is selected.
  NOT bundled into the binary.

* Ink — MIT — https://github.com/vadimdemedes/ink
  React renderer for the terminal.

* React — MIT — https://github.com/facebook/react

* Zod — MIT — https://github.com/colinhacks/zod

* gpt-tokenizer — MIT — https://github.com/niieani/gpt-tokenizer
  Pure-JS BPE tokenizer for cl100k_base.

* TypeScript — Apache-2.0 — https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript
  Dev dependency, not bundled.

================================================================================
Transitive LGPL notice
================================================================================

The @huggingface/transformers dependency tree may pull in `sharp` (a Node.js
image-processing library) which dynamically links libvips
(@img/sharp-libvips-*, LGPL-3.0-or-later).

PIPER itself does NOT use the sharp / libvips pipeline at runtime: the WASM
embedder runs ONNX models directly and never invokes the image-processing
path. The transitive dependency exists only because npm/bun resolve the full
graph at install time.

LGPL-3.0-or-later permits dynamic linking from a permissively-licensed work
without imposing copyleft on the wider distribution, provided the LGPL
component remains replaceable and its source is available. The libvips source
is available at: https://github.com/libvips/libvips

If your downstream use case requires a strict no-LGPL posture, you can:
1. Build PIPER without WASM embedding support (set embedding_backend=none or
   http in credentials.json).
2. Use `npm install --omit=optional` / `bun install --omit-optional` to skip
   the sharp binary platform packages.

